FamilyTree.person({"id":"bundervoet_petronella_1754","name":"Petronella Bundervoet","dates":"1754 – 1794","confidence":"sup","surname":"Bundervoet","family":"bundervoet","sex":"f","born":"1754","order":"1754","died":"1794","line":"bundervoet","father":"bundervoet_petrus_1727","mother":"dedeckere_marie_1731","siblings":[{"id":"bundervoet_annacatharina_1772","weak":true,"note":"rests on her Assenede birth in this household's own commune; no act names her parents"}],"spouses":[{"id":"vermeire_carolus","name":"Carolus Vermeire","detail":"m. Assenede, 1781"}],"source":"paulderidder; Assenede marriage 1781 — Carolus Vermeire × Petronella Bundervoet; Hontenisse death act nr. 31, 30 March 1814 — Maria Theresia Vermeeren","note":"One of the eight children of [[bundervoet_petrus_1727]] and [[dedeckere_marie_1731]]. Their father was\nthe younger brother of [[bundervoet_christoffel_1709]], through whom this tree's line runs, so these eight are\nfirst cousins of [[bundervoet_philippus_1756]]'s generation — blood relatives of Renée and Léon under\nobjective 2.\n\nRecorded first from the paulderidder tree's list of the couple's children, which gave two\nyears and nothing else. **She now has a marriage and a daughter.** The Assenede register\nhas her marriage to [[vermeire_carolus]] in **1781**; and a death act at Hontenisse, over\nthe border in Zeeuws-Vlaanderen, records their daughter [[vermeeren_maria_1787]] in 1814,\naged 27, born Assenede, naming her parents as Karel Vermeeren and Petronella Bundervoet.\n\nTwo independent documents in two countries name the same couple, and Assenede is where\nthis household already was — her father was buried there in 1777 and her sister\n[[bundervoet_coleta_1766]] was born there. That is what settles which Petronella\nBundervoet this is: the corpus holds at least two others of the same generation, a\nPetronella Judoca who married Jacobus Baudonck at Lotenhulle and a Petronille Thérèse who\nmarried André Lybaert at Evergem, and neither is at Assenede.\n\nIndex-level on both, so `sup`. The Hontenisse act has a scan that has not been read."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"bundervoet_petrus_1560","name":"Petrus Bundervoet","dates":"1560 – ~1605","confidence":"sup","surname":"Bundervoet","family":"bundervoet","born":"1560","order":"1560","died":"~1605","line":"bundervoet","spouses":[{"id":"vyveraert_nn","name":"NN Vyveraert"}],"source":"paulderidder; glorieuxp","note":"The earliest documented ancestor of the Bundervoet line (Evergem, ~1560); married twice and was the father of Livinus.\n\nCORRECTION (July 2026) to the note recorded a pass earlier. The full-text find was written\nup from search snippets as \"a Catherine Bundervoet widowed at Meirelbeke in 1587\" and a fief\nchain of 1687-1700. Reading the actual page ([[bundervoet-fiefs-alost-1687-1701]]) shows the\nsnippets had merged two adjacent entries: the Meirelbeke 1587 one belongs to the surname VAN\nDEN BUNDERE, not Bundervoet.\n\nWhat the page does show is a Bundervoet family at **Saint-Pierre-Alost**, holding fiefs from\n**1687 to 1701** — Catherine as widow and guardian in 1687, then Liévin son of François and\nLiévin son of Pasquier in 1700, and Pasquier son of Liévin in 1701. Descent stated, in the\nright century, some 30 km from Evergem. A lead for objective 3, and still not a link."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"bundervoet_petrus_1727","name":"Petrus (Pieter) Bundervoet","dates":"~1727 Evergem – 1777","confidence":"sup","surname":"Bundervoet","family":"bundervoet","born":"~1727 Evergem","order":"1727","died":"1777","line":"bundervoet","father":"bundervoet_joannes_1682","mother":"stockman_livina_1688","spouses":[{"id":"dedeckere_marie_1731","name":"Marie Anna de Deckere","detail":"m. ~1750"}],"source":"paulderidder","note":"Younger son of Joannes Bundervoet (~1682–1760) and Livina Stockman, and the brother of\nChristoffel (1709–1786) through whom our line descends. He existed in this tree only as\nthe string \"Pieter (1727)\" inside his father's note; every person referenced anywhere is\nsupposed to have their own record, and now he does.\n\nThe death is recorded as a burial, not a death: paulderidder gives \"deceased, aged about\n50\" with a burial at Assenede on 13 December 1777, and no date of death. The year is\nsafe, the day is the burial's, and the two are kept apart rather than collapsed into a\ndeath date the source never gave. Assenede, a little north of Evergem, is also the first\nsign of this branch moving off the home parish.\n\nHIS FAMILY IS NOW IN THE TREE — his wife [[dedeckere_marie_1731]] and all eight children. From\npaulderidder: married about 1750 to Marie Anna de Deckere (~1731–1809), with Joannis\n(1751), Livinus (1752), Petronella (1754–1794), Christophorus (1755), Joannis Baptista\n(1757), Livina Maria (1760–1831), Joanna (1761–1825) and Coleta (1766–1807). They are\nblood relatives of Renée and Léon — a great-uncle's whole household — and belong in the\ntree under objective 2. They are listed here rather than grafted because this pass took\nthe direct ancestors first, as the charter's priority requires.\n\nA second document sits on his paulderidder profile, captioned \"Petrus Bundervoet Akte\nvan teraardebestelling\" — a burial act. Not yet retrieved.\n\n**NOT PROVEN, refused — the Gent cluster (objective 3, 2026-07-27).** The harvested\ncorpus's own clustering (`coverage.surname_clusters()`) offers this record as the\n`best_link` for the biggest Gent-city cluster (153 mentions, 131 acts), on the strength of\na 30.7-bit, 1-independent-identifier score (name + kin-forename echo) against a \"Petrus\nBundervoet × Isabella De Coster\" whose son Joannes Baptista was born 1836 in Gent. NOT\nGRAFTABLE, labelled in `research/labels.jsonl`. Two independent grounds refuse it: the\nspouse named is Livina Quackebeke, not Marie Anna de Deckere; and the chronology is\nimpossible outright — that Petrus fathered sons in ~1790 and 1803, decades after this\nrecord's own attested 1777 death (buried Assenede). The Gent surname cluster in fact\nresolves into **six distinct, unrelated patriarch families** plus a 20th-century line\nalready independently attested — see research-log §73 for the full breakdown. None\nconnects here."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"bundervoet_petrus_1879","name":"Petrus Franciscus Bundervoet","dates":"19 Mar 1879 Evergem – 21 Jul 1943 Oostende","confidence":"sup","surname":"Bundervoet","family":"bundervoet","born":"19 Mar 1879 Evergem","order":"1879-03-19","died":"21 Jul 1943 Oostende","occupation":"werkman / meestergast","line":"bundervoet","father":"bundervoet_bernardus_1837","mother":"goethals_marie_1842","spouses":[{"id":"vanstechelman_augusta_1882","name":"Augusta Vanstechelman"}],"source":"Doodsprentjes.be memorial card — Pieter Franciscus Bundervoet, d. Oostende 21 July 1943; Familiekunde Vlaanderen Westkust memorial card — Pieter-Franciscus Bundervoet, d. Oostende 21 July 1943; Geneanet","note":"The FIRST of the Bundervoet line to leave Evergem for the coast: born in Evergem, he settled in Oostende, where all his children were born from 1905 onward. Married Augusta Vanstechelman (the Bundervoet–Vanstechelman junction). FRONTIER (MyHeritage, July 2026): two independent Bundervoet family trees on MyHeritage — one run by Lucien Bundervoet, one by Johny Henricus Bundervoet, both in Belgium — agree that he and Augusta had SIX children, not the one recorded here: Alphons Bernardus Cyprianus, Emilius Josephus, Achilles, Henricus Eduardus, Irena Augusta and Camillus Edmundus. The same trees give him at least four siblings, one named Maria Bundervoet. His own birth and death match ours exactly (19 Mar 1879 Evergem, 21 Jul 1943), which is what makes the match credible. NOT GRAFTED: member trees are not records and no dates for the children were readable, so the five siblings need their Oostende birth acts (1905 onward) before they go in.\n\nCORROBORATED (July 2026), and by two archives that do not share a source. Doodsprentjes.be\nand Familiekunde Vlaanderen Westkust each hold a memorial-card record for him, and both\ngive birth 19 March 1879 at Evergem, death 21 July 1943 at Oostende, and a Vanstechelman\npartner — the Familiekunde one naming her in full as Augusta. Four identifiers agreeing\nacross two independent collections, where before this record rested on a Geneanet tree.\n\nConfidence stays `sup` rather than rising to `doc`: these are transcriptions of memorial\ncards. The Doodsprentjes scan is behind a session — the record's image URL returns the\nsite's search page, not the card — so no image has been read here. Reading it, or the\nOostende 1943 civil death act, is what would make this `doc`."});
