FamilyTree.person({"id":"coekelberghs_anna_1853","name":"Anna Elisabeth Coekelberghs","dates":"b. 27 Jun 1853 Bertem","confidence":"sup","surname":"Coekelberghs","family":"coekelberghs","born":"27 Jun 1853 Bertem","order":"1853-06-27","line":"thumas","father":"coekelberghs_joannes_1809","mother":"haesaerts_anna_1810","source":"Civil birth acts naming both parents, for couples already in the tree (harvested corpus, July 2026)","note":"Named as the child in a civil birth act at **Bertem, 1853-06-27**, which gives both\nparents — [[coekelberghs_joannes_1809]] and [[haesaerts_anna_1810]] — and so places this person in the tree by the act rather than\nby a name.\n\nThe act is held in the harvested corpus and reached through Open Archives:\nhttps://www.openarchieven.nl/abl:15b96e6f-ada6-1154-e920-f9ed0a753c70\n\n`sup`: an indexed act, not the register image. **No `sex` is recorded**, because the harvested\nmention carries only the relation role and never a sex, and this project does not read a sex\noff a forename."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"coekelberghs_henricus","name":"Henricus Coekelberghs","dates":"d. after 1838","confidence":"sup","surname":"Coekelberghs","family":"coekelberghs","died":"after 1838","occupation":"landbouwer (farmer)","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"elseviers_petronella","name":"Petronella Elseviers"}],"source":"Bertem marriage register 1838, act nr. 8, 30 October 1838 — Coekelberghs × Haesaerts (REGISTER IMAGE); Bertem marriage act nr. 8, 30 October 1838 — Coeckelberghs × Haesaerts","note":"Father of the bridegroom in his son [[coekelberghs_joannes_1809]]'s marriage act — Bertem nr. 8,\n30 October 1838, **read as the register image**. A *landbouwer* of Berthem, and the act\nputs him there in person: *hier present en Consenteerende*. He is not called *wijlen*, so\nhe was alive that day, and he is given no age, so this record carries no birth date.\n\nLike everyone else named in the act except the witnesses — the bridegroom, the bride and\nall four parents — he declared he could not sign.\n\n**Why this record is `sup` although the image was read.** The image is `doc`-quality\nevidence for what the 1838 act *says*. It is not evidence that the groom it names is this\ntree's [[coekelberghs_joannes_1809]]. That bridge runs through the 1873 and 1903 acts, which are\nAGATHA transcriptions with no scan, so the chain is only as strong as its weakest link.\nSee docs/research-log.md §52 for why that distinction is now enforced, and §53 for this\npass.\n\nFRONTIER — his own marriage to [[elseviers_petronella]], which the Bertem registers should\nhold for roughly 1800–1809 and which would name his parents. The Bertem film that would\ncarry it is **DGS 004471767**, *Geboorten 1796–1870, Huwelijken 1796–1832, Overlijdens\n1796–1842* — the three record types are bound as separate blocks rather than interleaved,\nand there is no index over the film, so it is a browse rather than a lookup."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"coekelberghs_joannes_1809","name":"Joannes Baptista Coekelberghs","dates":"29 Jul 1809 Bertem – 19 Nov 1880 Bertem","confidence":"sup","surname":"Coekelberghs","family":"coekelberghs","born":"29 Jul 1809 Bertem","order":"1809-07-29","died":"19 Nov 1880 Bertem","occupation":"landbouwer (farmer)","line":"thumas","father":"coekelberghs_henricus","mother":"elseviers_petronella","spouses":[{"id":"haesaerts_anna_1810","name":"Anna Haesaerts"}],"source":"Bertem marriage register 1838, act nr. 8, 30 October 1838 — Coekelberghs × Haesaerts (REGISTER IMAGE); Bertem marriage act nr. 8, 30 October 1838 — Coeckelberghs × Haesaerts; Sint-Stevens-Woluwe marriage act nr. 6, 24 April 1873 — Van den Bemden × Coekelberghs; Kraainem marriage act nr. 16, 6 November 1903 — Van Esch × Coekelberghs, her remarriage","note":"Father of the bride in his daughter [[coekelberghs_maria_1848]]'s marriage act — Sint-Stevens-Woluwe\nnr. 6, 24 April 1873. A *landbouwer* of Bertem, present at the wedding and consenting to\nit, and recorded as unable to write or sign, *ongeletterd zijnde*.\n\nNamed a second time, thirty years later, in the same daughter's remarriage act — Kraainem\nnr. 16, 6 November 1903 — which is where his death comes from: Bertem, 19 November 1880.\nTwo acts three decades apart naming the same man as her father is what carries this link;\nthe 1873 act alone would be a surname and a commune.\n\n`sup` rather than `doc`: both are AGATHA act analyses — the Rijksarchief's own Demogen\ntranscriptions — and neither act has a scan on AGATHA or Open Archives.\n\nGRAFTED (2026-07-26) — his own marriage act, **Bertem nr. 8, 30 October 1838, read as the\nregister image** (`fs-bertem-1838-marriage-register-nr8`, ark `3:1:9392-HG83-X`). It gives\nhis birth, **Bertem 29 July 1809**, his trade at twenty-nine, *slagter*, and his parents\n[[coekelberghs_henricus]] and [[elseviers_petronella]] — both there in person, both\n*present en Consenteerende*, both unable to sign, as were the bridegroom, the bride and her\nparents. Banns 14 and 21 October; four witnesses, none stated as kin to either party. The\nimage also corrects AGATHA's spelling: the ink reads **Coekelberghs**, with no *c* before\nthe *k*.\n\n**The trade change was the objection, and it is now answered rather than waved away.**\nThe previous note here withheld the graft because he is a *slagter* in 1838, 1853 and 1867\nbut a *landbouwer* in 1858, 1861 and 1873. Two Bertem birth acts settle it: at his\ndaughter's birth on 27 June 1853 he declares himself in person, aged 44, born at Bertem,\n*slagter*; at his son's on 15 January 1858 he declares himself again, aged 48, born at\nBertem, *landbouwer*. The same man, self-declaring, on both sides of the change, five years\napart, in independently drawn acts — and both ages agree with a birth in July 1809. It is\none life, not two men, and an occupation was never a field that vetoes a match.\n\nThe rival reading was checked and refuted too. The only other Coekelberghs marriage\nindexed at Bertem in these years is *Joanna Catharina Coeckelberghs*, 1 February 1837 —\na woman born in 1797, daughter of an unrelated *Petrus* Coekelberghs who died in 1798. A\ncoincidence of surname, not a sibling and not a rival groom.\n\n**Still `sup`, and deliberately so.** The image proves what the 1838 act says. It does not\nprove that its bridegroom is the man the 1873 and 1903 acts name as\n[[coekelberghs_maria_1848]]'s father — that bridge runs entirely through two AGATHA transcriptions\nwith no scan behind them. A chain is as strong as its weakest link, and after\ndocs/research-log.md §52 this project says so rather than rounding up.\n\nFRONTIER — his own birth act would prove the 1809 date instead of reporting it, and his\ndeath act (Bertem, 19 November 1880) would give his age and confirm his parents. Both are\non FamilySearch film **DGS 004471767** (*Geboorten 1796–1870, Huwelijken 1796–1832,\nOverlijdens 1796–1842*), which is unindexed and bound in blocks by record type, so it is a\nbrowse. Better still: **Maria Theresia's own Bertem birth act of 22 November 1848** — that\none act would tie this 1838 couple directly to the 1848 daughter and move the whole cluster\noff the transcriptions."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"coekelberghs_joannes_1858","name":"Joannes Josephus Coekelberghs","dates":"b. 15 Jan 1858 Bertem","confidence":"sup","surname":"Coekelberghs","family":"coekelberghs","born":"15 Jan 1858 Bertem","order":"1858-01-15","line":"thumas","father":"coekelberghs_joannes_1809","source":"Civil birth acts naming both parents, for couples already in the tree (harvested corpus, July 2026)","note":"Named as the child in a civil birth act at **Bertem, 1858-01-15**, which names his father\nas *Joannes Baptista Coekelberghs* — [[coekelberghs_joannes_1809]], who declared the birth in person,\naged 48, born at Bertem — and so places this person in the tree by the act rather than by a\nname.\n\nThe act is held in the harvested corpus and reached through Open Archives:\nhttps://www.openarchieven.nl/abl:a0b8c9a0-322e-f254-e5f4-efcd42d1f8e0\n\n`sup`: an indexed act, not the register image. **No `sex` is recorded**, because the harvested\nmention carries only the relation role and never a sex, and this project does not read a sex\noff a forename.\n\nRETRACTED IN PART (2026-07-26): **the mother link has been removed.** This record was\nwritten with `mother: anna_haesaerts`, but the act does not name her. It names\n*Anna **Catharina** Haesaerts* — precisely the forename variant that §51 and §53\ndeliberately left **unmerged** with the plain *Anna Haesaerts* of the 1838, 1853, 1873 and\n1903 acts. Grafting this child to her would have silently resolved an open question by\nside effect, which is the way a tree acquires a decision nobody made.\n\nThe father side is not in doubt: he declares the birth himself, his age and birthplace\nagree with the 1838 marriage act read in §53, and Bertem is the commune. So the link\nupward stands and the link across does not.\n\nFRONTIER — one document settles it either way: the **1858 Bertem register image**, or\n[[haesaerts_anna_1810]]'s own death act, Bertem 8 January 1880. Until one is read, this record\nhas a father and no mother, and that is the honest shape of what is known."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"coekelberghs_maria_1848","name":"Maria Theresia Coekelberghs","dates":"22 Nov 1848 Bertem – 1919","confidence":"sup","surname":"Coekelberghs","family":"coekelberghs","born":"22 Nov 1848 Bertem","order":"1848-11-22","died":"1919","occupation":"herbergierster (innkeeper)","line":"thumas","father":"coekelberghs_joannes_1809","mother":"haesaerts_anna_1810","spouses":[{"id":"vandenbemden_henricus_1849","name":"Henricus Augustinus Vandenbemden","detail":"m. Sint-Stevens-Woluwe, 24 Apr 1873"},{"id":"vanesch_henricus_1836","name":"Henricus Van Esch","detail":"m. Kraainem, 6 Nov 1903"}],"source":"Kraainem marriage act nr. 2, 3 February 1902 — Thumas × Vandenbemden; Sint-Stevens-Woluwe marriage act nr. 6, 24 April 1873 — Van den Bemden × Coekelberghs; Kraainem marriage act nr. 16, 6 November 1903 — Van Esch × Coekelberghs, her remarriage; Geneanet","note":"Source detail: Geneanet; the 1902 Kraainem marriage act of her daughter Joanna\n\nJoanna Vandenbemden's mother; married Henricus Vandenbemden in 1873.\n\nDOCUMENTED (July 2026) as the bride's mother in the Kraainem marriage act of 3 February 1902 (akte nr. 2, [[S5]]): living at Kraainem,\n*herbergierster*, the occupation already held here. The act makes her the mother of\n[[vandenbemden_joanna_1882]] and the wife of [[vandenbemden_henricus_1849]].\n\nGRAFTED (2026-07-26): her own marriage act — **Sint-Stevens-Woluwe nr. 6, 24 April 1873** —\nnames her parents as [[coekelberghs_joannes_1809]], *landbouwer* of Bertem, and [[haesaerts_anna_1810]],\n*landbouwster* of Bertem, both present and both consenting. It also gives her birth to the\nday, **Bertem, 22 November 1848**, where this record held only the year; her trade at\ntwenty-four, *dienstmeid*; and the commune the marriage was passed in, which neither\nspouse's record carried.\n\nThe link does not rest on her name. Two identifiers already in this tree agree with the\nact exactly: the marriage date 24 April 1873, and her husband's birth — Everberg,\n19 August 1849, to the day. A third bridge pins her across thirty years: her **1903\nKraainem remarriage act** names the same parents again, and names her late first husband\nas *Henricus August Vandenbemden, overleden Kraainem 12/08/1889* — the same death date\n**and** the same commune that the 1902 act established when it corrected this tree's\nSint-Stevens-Woluwe to Kraainem. Three acts, thirty years apart, triangulating.\n\nThat 1903 act also gives her a second marriage this tree did not have, to the widower\n[[vanesch_henricus_1836]], and her parents' deaths: Bertem, 8 January and 19 November 1880.\n\nDOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`. Nothing about her was refuted — the evidence\ngrew. The demotion is about what the evidence *is*: every act behind this record, [[S5]]\nincluded, is an AGATHA act analysis, the Rijksarchief's own Demogen transcription, and not\none of them has a scan on AGATHA or Open Archives. `doc` means an act or its image was\nread. None was. See docs/research-log.md §51 — this record is one of about twenty carrying\nthe same overstatement, and the sweep is a pass of its own.\n\nFRONTIER — the register image for the 1873 act, which would move this whole cluster to\n`doc`. FamilySearch's microfilm for Sint-Stevens-Woluwe civil registration 1871–1875 is\ndigitised and browsable as DGS **004712751, item 2, images 317–768**; the specific image\nfor act nr. 6 has not been located within it. Her 1919 death is still a Geneanet claim and\nnothing found in this pass touches it."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"coenraets_amelia_1876","name":"Amelia Coenraets","dates":"b. 23 Apr 1876 Kraainem","confidence":"sup","surname":"Coenraets","family":"coenraets","born":"23 Apr 1876 Kraainem","order":"1876-04-23","occupation":"zonder beroep (no occupation)","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"vandenbemden_felix_1876","name":"Felix Van den Bemden","detail":"m. Kraainem, 8 Feb 1897"}],"source":"Marriage act — Felix Van den Bemden × Amelia Coenraets, Kraainem, 8 February 1897","note":"Named as the bride in her own marriage act, Kraainem, 8 February 1897, to [[vandenbemden_felix_1876]] —\nborn at Kraainem, 23 April 1876, *zonder beroep* (without occupation) at the time of the\nmarriage. The act names her parents as Franciscus Coenraets, *winkelier* of Kraainem, and Anna\nMaria Deridder, *winkelierster* of Kraainem — a shopkeeping couple, neither yet in this tree.\n\nThe act is held in the harvested corpus and reached through Open Archives:\nhttps://www.openarchieven.nl/abl:c122cfac-929f-f4a8-ff56-704f485d6171\n\n**Not the same person as [[coenraets_amelia_1903]]** (b. 1903, Sint-Stevens-Woluwe, daughter of\n[[coenraets_franciscus_1872]] and [[thumas_henrica_1878]]) — a different, later Amelia Coenraets in the same rare\nsurname's Kraainem/Sint-Stevens-Woluwe cluster. Noted explicitly so a future pass does not merge\nthe two by name alone.\n\n`sup`: an indexed act, not the register image. This record carries only what the marriage act\nstates; a person exists here so the marriage is a link rather than a string in someone else's\nnote."});
