FamilyTree.person({"id":"janssens_eric","name":"Eric Janssens","dates":"","confidence":"fam","surname":"Janssens","family":"janssens","sex":"m","born":"","line":"janssens","father":"janssens_rene","mother":"vancraenenbroeck_anna"});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"janssens_rene","name":"René Janssens","dates":"d. after 2000 Oostende","confidence":"fam","surname":"Janssens","family":"janssens","died":"after 2000 Oostende","line":"janssens","spouses":[{"id":"vancraenenbroeck_anna","name":"Anna Van Craenenbroeck"}],"source":"jswaelens","note":"A witloof (Belgian-endive / “wit goud”) farmer — the family grew the famous chicory around Zaventem (Vlaams-Brabant). René and Anna later moved to the coast (Middelkerke), and René died in Oostende (after 2000). His Janssens ancestry is not yet cracked — but NOT because of any hard privacy barrier. Belgium's civil-record publicity rule is graduated (births open at 100 yrs, marriages 75, deaths 50) AND, crucially, a direct descendant may obtain a relative's birth/marriage/death act at any age, so the documents that name René's parents ARE obtainable by the family. The reason online searching hasn't found him is mundane: (a) 'Janssens' is one of Belgium's commonest surnames, so with no anchor (his birth village or year, or a parent's name) his records can't be told apart from thousands of others; and (b) no descendant has published him in an online tree, and his post-2000 coast death notice isn't in the digitised obituary aggregators (inmemoriam, ingedachten, DELA all checked, July 2026). CONTRAST with his wife's side: Anna Van Craenenbroeck's Zaventem family WAS located (see anna_vc) because that surname is rare and village-specific. UNLOCKS for René: his birth village + approx year (enough to request the birth act and pull his parents), a bidprentje, or a relative's memory of his parents' names."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"joostens_guillaume","name":"Guillaume Joostens","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Joostens","family":"joostens","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"deconninck_jeanne","name":"Jeanne Marie Deconninck"}],"source":"Death act nr. 35 — Marie Catherine Joostens, Grez-Doiceau, 10 June 1857 (read on AGATHA); Death act of Marie Catherine Joostens, Grez-Doiceau, 10 June 1857","note":"Father of Marie Catharina Joostens (1810–1857), who married Georges Thumas — so a direct\nancestor through their son Georges Carolus Josephus.\n\nNamed, with his wife, in his daughter's Grez-Doiceau death act of 10 June 1857. That act is\nthe whole of what is known about him here: no dates, no place, no occupation, because it\ngave none. His daughter's birthplace is recorded there as Woluwe-Saint-Lambert, which is\nwhere this couple were most likely living around 1810, but the act does not say so and it\nis not recorded as a fact about him.\n\nFRONTIER: the Joostens–Deconninck marriage, and his own birth. Woluwe-Saint-Lambert and\nGrez-Doiceau are the two communes to try, roughly 1800–1810 for the marriage.\n\nACT READ (July 2026): named as the father of the deceased in his daughter's 1857\nGrez-Doiceau death act, read at the archive. He was added to this tree from an index entry a\npass earlier; this is the act behind it. It still gives him no dates, no place and no\noccupation, so none is recorded.\n\nDOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`, in the sweep of docs/research-log.md §52.\nNothing here was refuted and no fact changed. What changed is the honest name for the\nevidence: every act behind this record is an AGATHA act analysis — the Rijksarchief's own\nDemogen transcription — and AGATHA holds no scan for any of them. `doc` means an act or\nits image was actually read. None was. The register images are the named next step."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"joostens_marie_1810","name":"Marie Catharina Joostens","dates":"1810 – 10 Jun 1857 Grez-Doiceau","confidence":"sup","surname":"Joostens","family":"joostens","born":"1810","order":"1810","died":"10 Jun 1857 Grez-Doiceau","line":"thumas","father":"joostens_guillaume","mother":"deconninck_jeanne","spouses":[{"id":"thumas_georges_1804","name":"Georges Thumas","detail":"m. Grez-Doiceau, 17 Feb 1835"}],"source":"Death act nr. 35 — Marie Catherine Joostens, Grez-Doiceau, 10 June 1857 (read on AGATHA); Death act of Marie Catherine Joostens, Grez-Doiceau, 10 June 1857; Netradyle — Grez-Doiceau, actes de naissance (tab_naiss.php)","note":"DOCUMENTED (July 2026) by her own death act — Grez-Doiceau, 10 June 1857, the year this\nrecord already held — which gives two things the tree did not have at all: her **parents**,\nGuillaume Joostens and Jeanne Marie Deconninck, and her **birthplace**,\nWoluwe-Saint-Lambert. Her husband Georges Thumas is named as the survivor.\n\nHer parents are a documented frontier rather than a graft: they are named here plainly, and\nadding them is the obvious next step, but neither has a record yet and nothing beyond this\nact has been read about them.\n\nACT READ (July 2026). Her own death act — Grez-Doiceau, akte nr. 35, registered 11 June\n1857 and giving the death as the **10th** — read at the archive and saved\n([[joostens-1857-death-grez-doiceau]]). It fixes the day where this record had only the year,\nconfirms her birthplace of Woluwe-Saint-Lambert from the act itself, gives her occupation as\n*ménagère*, and names her parents [[joostens_guillaume]] and [[deconninck_jeanne]], who until\na pass ago were not in this tree at all.\n\nHer stated age of 47 puts her birth in 1809–10, which agrees with the 1810 held here.\n\nDOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`, in the sweep of docs/research-log.md §52.\nNothing here was refuted and no fact changed. What changed is the honest name for the\nevidence: every act behind this record is an AGATHA act analysis — the Rijksarchief's own\nDemogen transcription — and AGATHA holds no scan for any of them. `doc` means an act or\nits image was actually read. None was. The register images are the named next step.\n\nCONFLICT, RECORDED NOT RESOLVED (2026-07-27): [[netradyle-grez-naissances]]'s index of\nher son [[thumas_jean_1845]]'s 1845 birth gives her origin as **Woluwe-Saint-Pierre**, against the\n**Woluwe-Saint-Lambert** this record holds from her own 1857 death act (S9/S15). These are\ntwo distinct, adjacent communes, not a spelling variant of one place, and one\nunauthenticated 2006 volunteer index row does not outweigh her own act. Nothing changed;\nrecorded so a later pass does not silently pick one without knowing there was a choice."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"keirsebilck_joanna_1791","name":"Joanna Keirsebilck","dates":"24 May 1791 Oostkamp – 10 Jul 1866 Oostkamp","confidence":"sup","surname":"Keirsebilck","family":"keirsebilck","born":"24 May 1791 Oostkamp","order":"1791-05-24","died":"10 Jul 1866 Oostkamp","line":"bostyn-cappaert","father":"keirsebilck_petrus_1764","mother":"degrande_anna_1765","spouses":[{"id":"stroobandt_jacobus_1782","name":"Jacobus Stroobandt","detail":"m. Oostkamp, 24 Jul 1810"}],"source":"isavdw — Rijksarchief-sourced, with scan links; kathrynann","note":"Grandmother of Juliana Stroobandt. Outlived her husband by 45 years. Parents Petrus Josephus Keirsebilck × Anna Francisca De Grande."});
