FamilyTree.person({"id":"peperstraete_anne","name":"Anne Therese Peperstraete","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Peperstraete","family":"peperstraete","line":"van-iseghem","spouses":[{"id":"vincke_antonius","name":"Antonius Vincke","detail":"m. Westvleteren, 9 May 1797"}],"source":"bartvanhooren (Bart Vanhooren)","note":"Mother of Engel Antonius Vincke."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"peremans_cornelius","name":"Cornelius Peremans","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Peremans","family":"peremans","sex":"m","line":"thumas","father":"peremans_egidius","mother":"verelst_joanna","source":"Marriage banns of Cornelius Peremans, Zaventem, 21 April 1844","note":"A second sibling for [[peremans_joanna_1824]]. His marriage banns were published at **Zaventem on 21 April\n1844** (S30), naming his parents as [[peremans_egidius]] and *Theresia Verelst* — the short\nform of [[verelst_joanna]]'s name, which is how half this family's acts write her.\n\nThe timing is worth noticing. His father had been dead seven years and his mother **four\nmonths** — she died on 19 December 1843. He married in the spring after burying her, and his\nsister [[peremans_joanna_1824]] married in the February after that, at twenty-one and orphaned on both\nsides.\n\nAnchored the same way as [[peremans_maria]]: the record itself gives no place, and the\ncommune comes from the title of the volume the page sits in. Zaventem is where both his parents\ndied and where two of his sisters married.\n\n**His wife is not recorded, deliberately.** The index lists three more people in the same\ndocument — *Lambertus van der Pelen*, *Maria Catharina van der Vaeren* and *Maria Anna van der\nVaeren* — and states the role of none of them. Maria Anna is the likeliest bride and the other\ntwo the likeliest parents, but the surnames do not line up in the obvious way, and guessing\nwhich of three unlabelled people someone married is exactly the kind of invention this project\nforbids.\n\nFRONTIER: the marriage act itself, which would name the bride and both sets of parents; and his\nown birth act at Zaventem, probably in the 1810s or 1820s.\n\nWITNESS LINE, NOT A DATE (2026-07-27): he stands witness, aged 33, at his brother\n[[peremans_joannes_1820]]'s Zaventem marriage on 21 August 1848 (S34), which would imply a\nbirth around 1814–1815. Not written as a birth date here: a witness line carries only a\nname and a commune, with no parent names restated, which is *weaker* evidence than the\nparent-name pair that grafted him into this family in the first place. If this is ever\nrecorded, the only honest form is a `year_span` (`1814..1815`), never a point date."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"peremans_egidius","name":"Egidius Peremans","dates":"d. 6 Mar 1837 Zaventem","confidence":"sup","surname":"Peremans","family":"peremans","died":"6 Mar 1837 Zaventem","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"verelst_joanna","name":"Joanna Theresia Ver Elst"}],"source":"Zaventem marriage act nr. 1, 24 February 1846 — Bossin × Peremans; Marriage act — Joannes Baptista Peremans x Joanna Catharina Van Gindertaelen, Zaventem, 21 August 1848","note":"Father of the bride in his daughter [[peremans_joanna_1824]]'s marriage act — Zaventem, act nr. 1,\n24 February 1846 (S19), read as an image. He was dead by then, and the act gives the date\nand place exactly: Zaventem, 6 March 1837. His wife [[verelst_joanna]] outlived him by six\nyears.\n\nThe act gives him no age and no occupation. His daughter was 21 in February 1846, which\nplaces his own birth loosely in the 1780s or 1790s; nothing narrower is recorded.\n\nFRONTIER — his 1837 Zaventem death act, which would give his age, his occupation and\nvery probably his parents, and his marriage to Joanna Theresia Ver Elst, which the\nZaventem registers should hold for roughly 1815–1824. His daughter told the 1846 act that\nshe had never known her grandparents nor where they died, so his parents are not reachable\nthrough her.\n\nDOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`, in the sweep of docs/research-log.md §52.\nNothing here was refuted and no fact changed. What changed is the honest name for the\nevidence: every act behind this record is an AGATHA act analysis — the Rijksarchief's own\nDemogen transcription — and AGATHA holds no scan for any of them. `doc` means an act or\nits image was actually read. None was. The register images are the named next step.\n\nA FIFTH CHILD's marriage act names him again (2026-07-27): [[peremans_joannes_1820]]\nmarried at Zaventem on 21 August 1848 (S34), and that act gives his own death as\n**1836-03-06** — the same day and month as the 1837-03-06 held above from S19, one year\napart. **Both dates are recorded here, and neither is picked.** 1837-03-06 comes from\nS19 (his daughter [[peremans_joanna_1824]]'s 1846 marriage act); 1836-03-06 comes from S34 (his son\n[[peremans_joannes_1820]]'s 1848 marriage act). Both are AGATHA/Demogen index\ntranscriptions of the civil registration — neither is a register image. Positing two\nseparate Egidius Peremans × Joanna Theresia Verelst couples in Zaventem, whose wives\nhappen to share an identical day-level death date (see [[verelst_joanna]]), is a far\nlarger coincidence than a single-digit transcription slip in one of the two independent\nDemogen batches — so this is read as one man, not two. But which digit slipped is not\ndecided by that reasoning, and the frontmatter `death.date` field is left at 1837-03-06,\nthe earlier-held value, rather than silently overwritten by the later one. Only the\nZaventem 1836/1837 death register image — or the act itself — settles which year is\ncorrect."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"peremans_joanna_1824","name":"Joanna Catharina Jacoba Peremans","dates":"1824 Zaventem – 1907","confidence":"doc","surname":"Peremans","family":"peremans","born":"1824 Zaventem","order":"1824","died":"1907","occupation":"huishoudster (housekeeper)","line":"thumas","father":"peremans_egidius","mother":"verelst_joanna","spouses":[{"id":"bossin_guilielmus_1824","name":"Guilielmus Bossin","detail":"m. Zaventem, 24 Feb 1846"}],"source":"Marriage of Joannes Athanasius Peremans x Maria Elisabeth Cuypers, Zaventem, November 1844; Marriage banns of Cornelius Peremans, Zaventem, 21 April 1844; Marriage of Petrus van der Varent x Maria Josephina Peremans, Zaventem, 5 November 1840; Zaventem marriage act nr. 1, 24 February 1846 — Bossin × Peremans; Kraainem marriage act nr. 2, 20 June 1872 — Thumas × Bossin (read on AGATHA); Marriage act — Georges Carolus Josephus Thumas × Antonia Bossin, Kraainem, 20 June 1872; Civil registration acts for the Bossin family of Sint-Stevens-Woluwe, Kraainem and Zaventem (harvested corpus, July 2026); Marriage act — Joannes Baptista Peremans x Joanna Catharina Van Gindertaelen, Zaventem, 21 August 1848; Civil marriage acts for the Bossin/Swaelens/Vrebos family of Kraainem, 1884-1901 (harvested corpus, July 2026); Marriage act nr. 3 — Joannes Baptista Julianus Swaelens x Ludovica Bossin, Kraainem, 25 February 1884 (register image); Marriage act nr. 6 — Henricus Bossin x Maria Catharina Guns, Kraainem, 23 November 1887 (register image); Marriage banns nrs. 7 and 8 — Joannes Baptista Julianus Swaelens x Ludovica Bossin, Alsemberg, 10 and 17 February 1884 (register image)","note":"CORROBORATED (July 2026) as a mother, by her daughter's Kraainem marriage act of\n20 June 1872, which names the bride's parents as Guilielmus Bossin and *Anna* Catharina\nPeremans. The act's forenames are shorter than the ones this record carries — Anna\nCatharina against Joanna Catharina Jacoba — which is ordinary for a civil act quoting a\nparent, but it is a difference, and it is recorded rather than smoothed over.\n\nACT READ (July 2026): named in her daughter's 1872 marriage act as *huishoudster*, living\nat Kraainem. The act calls her Anna Catharina; this record calls her Joanna Catharina\nJacoba. Both stand.\n\nACT READ (2026-07-26): her own marriage act — Zaventem nr. 1, 24 February 1846 (S19),\nread as an image. It **settles the forename dispute above in this record's favour**: the\nact that married her calls her *Joanna Catharina Jacoba Peremans*, in full, which is what\nthis tree held. The shorter *Anna Catharina* of her daughter's 1872 act was the act\nquoting a parent from memory, and it is now the outlier rather than a rival reading.\n\nShe was **born at Zaventem** and was **21**, which confirms the 1824 from a civil act and\nmatches her husband year for year. Her occupation at marriage was *arbeidster*.\n\nThe act names her parents [[peremans_egidius]] and [[verelst_joanna]], both already dead\n— he at Zaventem on 6 March 1837, she at Zaventem on 19 December 1843 — so she married at\ntwenty-one an orphan on both sides. And it records something registers rarely bother with:\nshe declared, with her witnesses, that **she had never known her grandparents nor where\nthey died**. That is a documented dead end, not a gap to keep searching by name.\n\nFRONTIER — three probable siblings, named and not grafted (2026-07-26). FamilySearch's index\nof the Brabant civil registration returns, for *Egidius Peremans*, several rows giving his\nspouse as **Joanna Theresia ver Elst** and a child other than her: **Maria Josephina\nPeremans**, **Joannes Athanasius Peremans**, and a **Cornelius Peremans** whose mother is given\nin the shorter form *Theresia Verelst*.\n\nThe parent pair matches [[peremans_egidius]] and [[verelst_joanna]] exactly, and *Athanasius*\nis rare enough to be worth something on its own. They are still not grafted, for a specific\nreason: **none of those rows carries an event date or a place.** Rule 1 wants two independent\nidentifiers with one of them anchored, and a parent pair with no commune and no year is not\nanchored. The same search, narrowed by parent, then drifted to a quite different family of\nPeremans in Antwerp — FamilySearch's parent filters are advisory, which this log has recorded\nbefore — so the rows need opening one by one before any of them becomes a person here.\n\nONE OPENED (2026-07-26), and it anchored. **Maria Josephina** married *Petrús van der Varent*\non **5 November 1840** — and the place, which the indexed record does not carry, comes from the\ntitle of the volume the page sits in: ***Zaventem. Huwelijksakten 1840***. Zaventem is where\nthis record's father died, where her mother died, and where she herself was born and married.\nPlace plus an exact parent pair is two identifiers with one anchored, so [[peremans_maria]]\nis now in the tree as her sister, with a husband and his parents.\n\nTWO OPENED (2026-07-26), both anchored the same way. **[[peremans_cornelius]]** had his\nmarriage banns published at **Zaventem on 21 April 1844** (S30) — four months after his mother\ndied and seven years after his father — with the parent pair given in the short form *Egidius\nPeremans × Theresia Verelst*. His bride is deliberately not recorded: the index lists three\nmore people in that document and states the role of none of them.\n\n**[[peremans_joannes_1822]]** is now open too, and his entry is the best of the three: it\nlabels the roles outright — *Vader* Egidius Peremans, *Moeder* Joanna Theresia ver Elst — so\nnothing is inferred. Born about 1822, he married *Maria Elisabeth Cuypers* at **Zaventem**,\nbanns on 17 and 24 November 1844, registered on the 28th (S31). Her parents come with her.\n\n**All three siblings are now in, and together they say something.** Their father died in March\n1837 and their mother on 19 December 1843. Cornelius's banns went up in April 1844, Joannes\nAthanasius's in November 1844, and this record's own marriage followed in February 1846 —\nthree weddings in the twenty-six months after their mother's death, all in the same commune.\nWhether that is grief, or the family council an orphaned minor needed under the Code, or simply\nthree young people who had been waiting, the acts do not say. The pattern is recorded; the\ncause is not.\n\n**And the 1840 act points at what §47 could not reach.** [[peremans_egidius]] was already dead\nin November 1840, so marrying his daughter required an extract of his death act, bound into\nthat volume as a *huwelijksbijlage* — the exact mechanism that produced [[S23]]. Vlaams-Brabant\nindexes no death acts at all, which makes the annexes to this 1840 marriage the best route to\nhis 6 March 1837 act, and they are one volume away.\n\nDOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`, in the sweep of docs/research-log.md §52.\nNothing here was refuted and no fact changed. What changed is the honest name for the\nevidence: every act behind this record is an AGATHA act analysis — the Rijksarchief's own\nDemogen transcription — and AGATHA holds no scan for any of them. `doc` means an act or\nits image was actually read. None was. The register images are the named next step.\n\nA FOURTH CHILD (2026-07-27): [[bossin_joannes_1851]], born Sint-Stevens-Woluwe 15 August\n1851, between [[bossin_antonia_1849]] (1849) and [[bossin_ludovica_1853]] (1853) — she is aged 27 on that\nact, born Zaventem, matching this record's 1824 birth year and birthplace exactly. The\nact calls her *Anna Catharina Peremans*, the shortened form already reconciled above: her\nown 1846 marriage act gives the full *Joanna Catharina Jacoba* this record holds, so a\nlater act repeating the shorter form is a clerk's memory of that document, not a rival\nname needing its own resolution. **That is the precedent this project needs stated\nplainly, because it recurs**: a shortened form is a settled variant, not an open question,\nwhenever an *independent* document already in hand — not the disputed act itself — gives\nthe full form. It is exactly what distinguishes this case from\n[[haesaerts_anna_1810]]'s: her own record is called plain \"Anna\" on the act that anchors her in\nthis tree, and \"Anna Catharina\" appears only inside the disputed acts themselves — so no\ndocument independent of the dispute has ever settled it, and grafting on the strength of\nthe disputed act would use it to settle its own admissibility. Here the anchor (S19) and\nthe dispute are different documents; there, they are the same one.\n\nShe appears again, aged 29, at [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s own birth act (Sint-Stevens-Woluwe\nnr. 18, 5 October 1853, S33) — again *Anna Catharina Peremans*, born Zaventem, matching\nher 1824 birth year exactly.\n\nA FIFTH SIBLING (2026-07-27): [[peremans_joannes_1820]] married **Joanna Catharina Van\nGindertaelen** at Zaventem on **21 August 1848** (S34). This one clears a higher bar than\nthe three found in 2026-07-26: it carries an exact day-level match to their mother's\nalready-held death date (19 December 1843, Zaventem) from a *separately transcribed*\nregister, not merely the parent-name pair with a place borrowed from a volume title. The\nsame act also surfaces a one-year conflict on their father's own death date — 1836 here\nagainst 1837 in this record's own S19 — left open, and recorded in full on\n[[peremans_egidius]] rather than picked here.\n\nA FIFTH AND SIXTH CHILD (2026-07-27): [[bossin_henricus_1863]] (b. Kraainem, 1 January 1863)\nand [[bossin_joannes_1866]] (b. Kraainem, 17 March 1866), from their own 1887\nKraainem and 1896 Werchter marriage acts. Both name this record as *Joanna Catharina\nPeremans*, landbouwster of Kraainem — the fuller form, closer to the S19 anchor's *Joanna\nCatharina Jacoba* than the *Anna Catharina* several other acts in this family use — again\nthe already-reconciled variant, not a new dispute.\n\nTwo of her daughter [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s acts corroborate this record twice more: her own\n1884 marriage act (*Anna Catharina Peremans*) and her 1901 remarriage act (*Anna Catharina\nPeremans*, again zonder beroep) — the shortened form, matching the pattern already\ndocumented above.\n\n**TWO REGISTER IMAGES READ (2026-07-27), and a THIRD forename variant surfaces.** The\nKraainem marriage act and the Alsemberg banns behind [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s 1884 wedding\nwere both read as images, not indexes, and between them this record's mother appears in\n**three different forms across three documents**: ***Joanna Maria*** on the Alsemberg\nbanns (both act nr. 7 and act nr. 8), ***Anna Catharina*** on the Kraainem marriage act\neight days later — where she is **present in person, signs \"A.C. Peremans\"**, confirmed by\ndirect inspection of the signature line — and ***Joanna Catharina*** on her son\n[[bossin_henricus_1863]]'s 1887 Kraainem marriage act, where she again signs, this time\n**\"C. Peremans\"**. One woman's own signature in two different forms, three years apart, in\naddition to the *Joanna Catharina Jacoba* of her own 1846 marriage act (S19) and the *Anna\nCatharina* the family's several other acts already use.\n\n**This still holds together as one woman, and §65's ruling is explicitly NOT reopened.**\nWhat anchors her identity is not any of the disputed acts themselves but two things\nindependent of all of them: her husband, named identically as **Guilielmus/Guillelmus\nBossin** across every one of these documents, and her daughter [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s own\n**exact day-level birth date and place** (5 October 1853, Sint-Stevens-Woluwe), restated\nwithout variation across her birth act, her 1884 marriage act and her 1901 remarriage act.\nThat is precisely what distinguishes this case from [[haesaerts_anna_1810]]'s, where no\ndocument independent of the disputed acts ever settled her forename: there, the anchor and\nthe dispute are the same acts; here, the anchor (S19, plus the daughter's birth) sits\noutside the dispute entirely, so a third spelling variant is one more clerk's rendering of\nthe same well-anchored woman, not a new question to resolve."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"peremans_joannes_1820","name":"Joannes Baptista Peremans","dates":"b. ~1820","confidence":"sup","surname":"Peremans","family":"peremans","sex":"m","born":"~1820","order":"1820","occupation":"arbeider (labourer)","line":"thumas","father":"peremans_egidius","mother":"verelst_joanna","spouses":[{"id":"vangindertaelen_joanna_1825","name":"Joanna Catharina Van Gindertaelen","detail":"m. Zaventem, 21 Aug 1848"}],"source":"Marriage act — Joannes Baptista Peremans x Joanna Catharina Van Gindertaelen, Zaventem, 21 August 1848","note":"A fifth sibling for [[peremans_joanna_1824]]. He married **Joanna Catharina Van Gindertaelen** at\n**Zaventem on 21 August 1848** (S34), banns published on 6 and 13 August. He was **28**,\nan *arbeider*, and the act states he could not write and had already completed his\nmilitia obligations; she was **23**, an *arbeidster*, also unable to write. Twenty-eight\nin August 1848 gives the *about 1820* birth year here — an age converted to a year and\nmarked approximate, not a date the act itself gives.\n\n**Two independent identifiers anchor the parent link**, not one. First: the act names his\nmother as *Joanna Theresia Peremans* — read [[verelst_joanna]] — already dead, at\n**Zaventem, 19 December 1843**, an exact day-level match to the death date this tree\nalready holds for her, drawn from a *separately transcribed* Demogen batch (S19, his\nsister's 1846 marriage act, a different register entirely). Second: his father is named\nas *Egidius Peremans*, matching [[peremans_egidius]] by name and commune. A day-level\ndeath date repeated identically across two independent transcriptions of two different\nregisters is not the kind of thing two unrelated Egidius Peremans × Joanna Theresia\nVerelst couples would coincidentally share.\n\n**This clears a higher bar than the three siblings already in the tree.**\n[[peremans_cornelius]], [[peremans_joannes_1822]] and [[peremans_maria]] were each\ngrafted on the parent-name pair alone, with the commune supplied only by the title of the\nvolume the indexed page sat in, and with no exact date anywhere in the record. This act\ngives an exact day-level date on one of the two parents, independently corroborating a\nfact already held rather than merely repeating an unanchored name.\n\nHer parents are named directly in the act, with their roles labelled, so there is no\nidentity question to resolve: **Joannes Franciscus Van Gindertaelen** (present and\nconsenting) × **Anna Catharina Huenaerts** (present and consenting), both *arbeider(ster)*\nof Zaventem — see [[vangindertaelen_joannes]] and [[huenaerts_anna]].\n\n**Four witnesses stood at the wedding.** Two are already-held siblings and add little:\n[[peremans_cornelius]], 33 (implying a birth around 1814–1815, recorded on his own record\nas a witness-line lead, not a date), and [[peremans_joannes_1822]], 26 (consistent with\nhis already-held ~1822, adding no precision). A third, **Petrus Van Der Varen**, 35, is a\nplausible but unconfirmed match for [[peremans_maria]]'s husband\n[[vandervarent_petrus_1813]] — the surname is spelled differently here, the act states no relation,\nand the reasoning is recorded on his own record as a frontier, not a link. The fourth,\n**Franciscus Engels**, 38, *veldwachter* (rural constable), is not a family lead at all: he\nis a recurring professional witness, not kin — see the note on that below.\n\n**A conflict, not smoothed over.** This act gives Egidius Peremans's own death as\n**1836-03-06** — one year off the **1837-03-06** already held from S19. Both dates and\nthe reasoning for leaving the question open are recorded on [[peremans_egidius]] itself,\nnot resolved here.\n\n**A recurring non-candidate.** *Franciscus Engels, veldwachter*, also witnesses\n[[peremans_joanna_1824]]'s own 1846 marriage act at Zaventem, aged 35 there and 38 here — the two ages\nintersect cleanly at a birth in Feb–Aug 1810, consistent with one man holding a single\nper-commune post across both weddings. No person file is warranted for a recurring\nprofessional witness.\n\nFRONTIER — his own birth act at Zaventem around 1820, and any children of this marriage.\nNo search has been made for a rival Egidius Peremans × Joanna Theresia Verelst couple\nelsewhere in Vlaams-Brabant: the identical day-level death date made it seem unnecessary,\nbut that is a judgement, not a search, and it is recorded here as unchecked rather than\nas checked.\n\n`sup`: an index-level AGATHA/Demogen act analysis, no register image read. A FamilySearch\nscan exists (`ark:/61903/3:1:S3HY-6423-8F1?i=24`) but is behind a login wall — confirmed\n`blocked` this pass, a one-request retry, not yet a scoped miss."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"peremans_joannes_1822","name":"Joannes Athanasius Peremans","dates":"b. ~1822","confidence":"sup","surname":"Peremans","family":"peremans","sex":"m","born":"~1822","order":"1822","line":"thumas","father":"peremans_egidius","mother":"verelst_joanna","spouses":[{"id":"cuypers_maria","name":"Maria Elisabeth Cuypers","detail":"m. Zaventem, 28 Nov 1844"}],"source":"Marriage of Joannes Athanasius Peremans x Maria Elisabeth Cuypers, Zaventem, November 1844","note":"The third sibling found for [[peremans_joanna_1824]], and the eldest of them — born about **1822**, two\nyears before her. He married **Maria Elisabeth Cuypers** at **Zaventem**, banns on 17 and 24\nNovember 1844 and the marriage registered on the **28th** (S31).\n\n**This entry is better than the other two.** Where Cornelius's record left three people\nunlabelled, this one names the roles outright: *Vader* [[peremans_egidius]], *Moeder*\n[[verelst_joanna]], the spouse, and her parents [[cuypers_antonius]] and [[dehose_anna]]. Nothing about\nthe family's shape has to be inferred.\n\nThe birth year is approximate because it is FamilySearch's estimate from an age, not a date\nfrom an act — hence `~1822` and not a day.\n\n*Athanasius* is a rare enough forename to search on alone, which is what makes him easier to\nfollow than his brother.\n\nFRONTIER: his own birth act at Zaventem around 1822, and the marriage act itself, which would\ngive both trades and his exact age.\n\nHe stands witness, aged 26, at his brother [[peremans_joannes_1820]]'s Zaventem marriage on\n21 August 1848 (S34) — consistent with the ~1822 already held here, but adding no\nprecision of its own. Recorded for completeness; nothing here changes."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"peremans_maria","name":"Maria Josephina Peremans","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Peremans","family":"peremans","sex":"f","line":"thumas","father":"peremans_egidius","mother":"verelst_joanna","spouses":[{"id":"vandervarent_petrus_1813","name":"Petrús van der Varent","detail":"m. Zaventem, 5 Nov 1840"}],"source":"Marriage of Petrus van der Varent x Maria Josephina Peremans, Zaventem, 5 November 1840","note":"The first sibling this tree has for [[peremans_joanna_1824]]. She married **Petrús van der Varent** at\n**Zaventem on 5 November 1840** (S29), her parents given as [[peremans_egidius]] and\n[[verelst_joanna]] — the same pair, with the same distinctive three-part maternal name, that\nher sister's 1846 act gives.\n\nHer father had been dead three and a half years by then; her mother had three years to live.\nHer sister married in the same commune six years later.\n\nNo birth date: the record gives her none, not even an age, so this record has none either.\n\n`sup`: an indexed registration. The act's own words have not been read — only the\ntranscription and the register page it sits on.\n\nFRONTIER: her own birth act, and any children of the van der Varent marriage."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"pergijsels_maria","name":"Maria Anna Pergijsels","dates":"d. after 1853","confidence":"sup","surname":"Pergijsels","family":"pergijsels","died":"after 1853","occupation":"dagloonster (day labourer)","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"pardon_guilielmus","name":"Guilielmus Pardon"}],"source":"Marriage act nr. 9 — Françiscus Pardon × Anna Maria Bossin, Sint-Stevens-Woluwe, 1 December 1853","note":"Mother of the groom in her son [[pardon_franciscus_1830]]'s marriage act — Sint-Stevens-Woluwe\nnr. 9, 1 December 1853 (S22). A *dagloonster* of Winksele, present and consenting, and\nlike her husband [[pardon_guilielmus]] recorded as unable to write.\n\nThe act gives her no age, so this record carries no birth date, and the death entry says\nonly that she was alive on the day.\n\nThe surname is a rare one and its spelling is unsettled — *Pergijsels* is what the act\nwrites, and Pergijssels, Pergisels and Peregijsels all occur in Brabant. That rarity is\nworth more than it looks: a surname this uncommon is exactly the kind that can be searched\non its own, where Bossin and Pardon cannot."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"perquy_anna_1806","name":"Anna Theresia Perquy","dates":"b. 9 Jul 1806 Westkerke","confidence":"sup","surname":"Perquy","family":"perquy","born":"9 Jul 1806 Westkerke","order":"1806-07-09","line":"bostyn-cappaert","father":"perquy_joannes","mother":"stekelorum_agatha","spouses":[{"id":"bostin_henricus_1801","name":"Henricus Josephus Bostin","detail":"m. Westkerke, 9 May 1833"}],"source":"stefanieschil; Vrijwilligers RAB","note":"Mother of Henricus Josephus Bostyn (1841). Youngest of the Perquy children (Petrus 1795, Victoria Francisca 1798, Engelbertus, Pieter)."});
