FamilyTree.person({"id":"swaelens_egidius","name":"Egidius Swaelens","dates":"","confidence":"doc","surname":"Swaelens","family":"swaelens","sex":"m","occupation":"hovenier (gardener)","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"ackermans_joanna","name":"Joanna Ackermans"}],"source":"Civil marriage acts for the Bossin/Swaelens/Vrebos family of Kraainem, 1884-1901 (harvested corpus, July 2026); Marriage act nr. 3 — Joannes Baptista Julianus Swaelens x Ludovica Bossin, Kraainem, 25 February 1884 (register image); Marriage banns nrs. 7 and 8 — Joannes Baptista Julianus Swaelens x Ludovica Bossin, Alsemberg, 10 and 17 February 1884 (register image)","note":"Named as father of the groom in his son [[swaelens_joannes_1858]]'s marriage act — Kraainem, 25\nFebruary 1884 — resident **Braine-l'Alleud**, *hovenier*. The act gives him no age and no\ndates, so this record has none.\n\n**UPGRADED TO `doc` (2026-07-27) — no longer single-source.** The Kraainem act's own\nregister image was read: he was **present in person and consenting**, and the act states\nhe **could not sign, being illiterate** (\"verklaart niet te kunnen teekenen, ongeletterd\nzijnde\"), a detail only the image carries. A second, independent register corroborates\nhim before the act existed at all — the **Alsemberg marriage banns**, nrs. 7 and 8 (10 and\n17 February 1884), a different commune, a different register series and a different\nclerk, name the same pair: *Egidius Swaelens, hovenier, wonende te Braken-Eigen\n[Eigenbrakel = Braine-l'Alleud]*. Two independent identifiers — occupation (*hovenier*)\nand residence (Braine-l'Alleud) — now agree across two unconnected registers, which is\nwhat lifts this record off the single-act standard it was accepted at (see\n[[bossin_cornelius_1847]], [[bossin_joannes_1851]] and [[bossin_antonia_1849]] (Antonia) for that standard).\n\nFRONTIER: his marriage to [[ackermans_joanna]], and his own birth and death."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"swaelens_franciscus","name":"Franciscus Swaelens","dates":"b. 1859–1860","confidence":"doc","surname":"Swaelens","family":"swaelens","sex":"m","born":"1859–1860","order":"1859","occupation":"schrijnwerker (carpenter)","line":"thumas","father":"swaelens_egidius","mother":"ackermans_joanna","source":"Marriage act nr. 3 — Joannes Baptista Julianus Swaelens x Ludovica Bossin, Kraainem, 25 February 1884 (register image)","note":"**A REFUSAL ANSWERED BY THE DOCUMENT (2026-07-27).** He stands witness at his brother\n[[swaelens_joannes_1858]]'s wedding — Kraainem, 25 February 1884 — aged 24, resident\n**Sint-Genesius-Rode**, a *schrijnwerker*, the groom's own trade. Research-log §72 found\nhim in the same act and explicitly refused to graft him as a relative: a shared surname\nplus a shared trade is one correlated signal, not the two independent identifiers Rule 1\nrequires, since a trade runs in families and apprenticeships. **The register image, read\nthis pass, states the relationship outright and removes the need to infer it**: *\"Franciscus\nSwaelens, schrijnwerker, oud vier en twintig jaren, wonende te Sint-Genesius Rhode,\n**broeder des bruidegoms**\"* — brother of the groom, in the act's own words. He also\nsigns the register himself (\"Fs Swaelens\").\n\nThat makes him a son of [[swaelens_egidius]] and [[ackermans_joanna]], alongside\n[[swaelens_joannes_1858]]. No birth date is stated on the act — only his age, 24, on 25 February 1884 —\nso this record holds the year span that age permits, **1859..1860**, and no point year.\n\n**The methodological point, not just the outcome.** §72's refusal was the right call on\nthe evidence then in hand: no act named a relationship, and the surname-plus-trade\ncorrelation is exactly the kind of single, non-independent signal Rule 1 exists to keep\nout of the tree. It was overturned by reading the primary document itself, not by a better\nscore or a change of mind — the loop working as designed, in both directions.\n\nFRONTIER: his own birth and marriage, and whether he too settled in Kraainem or stayed at\nSint-Genesius-Rode."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"swaelens_joannes_1858","name":"Joannes Baptista Julianus Swaelens","dates":"11 May 1858 Alsemberg – 7 Oct 1898 Kraainem","confidence":"doc","surname":"Swaelens","family":"swaelens","sex":"m","born":"11 May 1858 Alsemberg","order":"1858-05-11","died":"7 Oct 1898 Kraainem","occupation":"schrijnwerker (carpenter)","line":"thumas","father":"swaelens_egidius","mother":"ackermans_joanna","spouses":[{"id":"bossin_ludovica_1853","name":"Ludovica Bossin","detail":"m. Kraainem, 25 Feb 1884"}],"source":"Birth declarations at Sint-Stevens-Woluwe for three children of Guilielmus Bossin x Peremans, 1847-1853; Civil marriage acts for the Bossin/Swaelens/Vrebos family of Kraainem, 1884-1901 (harvested corpus, July 2026); Marriage act nr. 3 — Joannes Baptista Julianus Swaelens x Ludovica Bossin, Kraainem, 25 February 1884 (register image); Marriage banns nrs. 7 and 8 — Joannes Baptista Julianus Swaelens x Ludovica Bossin, Alsemberg, 10 and 17 February 1884 (register image); Marriage act nr. 6 — Henricus Bossin x Maria Catharina Guns, Kraainem, 23 November 1887 (register image)","note":"Married [[bossin_ludovica_1853]] and, through her, is [[bossin_antonia_1849]]'s brother-in-law. This\nrecord previously held nothing beyond that marriage and the bride's parents, from S24's\nbare index row. It is filled in (2026-07-27) from the civil marriage act itself.\n\n**Own marriage act, Kraainem, 25 February 1884** — a fuller record of the same wedding\nS24 already carried, not a replacement of it (see the date/place note below). It states\nhis birth directly: **Alsemberg, 11 May 1858**, and his trade, *schrijnwerker*. His\nparents are named: **Egidius Swaelens**, hovenier of Braine-l'Alleud, and **Joanna\nAckermans**, already dead — Alsemberg, 27 December 1877 — see [[swaelens_egidius]] and\n[[ackermans_joanna]].\n\n**Corroborated internally**: standing witness at [[bossin_henricus_1863]]'s wedding — Kraainem,\n23 November 1887 — he is given as 29, *schrijnwerker*, resident Kraainem by then. Age 29\non that date is an exact arithmetic match to the 1858-05-11 birth this act gives, and\nshows he had relocated to his wife's commune within three years of marrying.\n\n**Death dated (2026-07-27)**: named as [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s deceased \"vorige partner\nbruid\" in her 1901 remarriage act — Kraainem, 30 May 1901 — which gives his death as\n**Kraainem, 7 October 1898**. Two identifiers make this unambiguous rather than a\nnamesake's death: the rare triple-forename-plus-surname combination, and the bride named\nalongside him in that same act is independently confirmed as this tree's\n[[bossin_ludovica_1853]] by an exact match on her own birth date and place.\n\n**THE REGISTER IMAGES WERE READ (2026-07-27), and both this record and its confidence are\nupgraded from `sup` to `doc`.** Two things previously left open close.\n\n**The 17-vs-25 February discrepancy is SETTLED, not merely reconciled by inference.**\nBoth documents now say it outright, not just plausibly. The Kraainem marriage act states,\nin its own words, that the banns were published \"op de Zondagen tienden en zeventienden\nFebruari laatstleden\" — the tenth and seventeenth of February — at Kraainem and Alsemberg.\nIndependently, the **Alsemberg banns register itself** was read: acts nrs. 7 and 8, each\nprinted **AKTE VAN HUWELIJKSAFKONDIGING** (a banns publication, not a marriage), dated\nSunday 10 February (\"voor de eerste maal afgekondigd\") and Sunday 17 February (\"voor de\ntweede maal afgekondigd\") 1884. **S24's \"17 February 1884, Alsemberg\" was never a wrong\ndate — it was the second banns publication, correctly read off the FamilySearch index and\nthen misread here as a marriage date.** The marriage itself is **25 February 1884,\nKraainem**, now the value this record and [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s carry, in agreement as the\ndata model requires; the two banns dates are recorded here, in prose, rather than\ndiscarded, since they are a real and dated event, just not this one.\n\n**Franciscus Swaelens is the groom's brother — the act states it, closing a refusal this\ntree made on the strength of inference alone.** The witness clause reads: *\"Franciscus\nSwaelens, schrijnwerker, oud vier en twintig jaren, wonende te Sint-Genesius Rhode,\n**broeder des bruidegoms**\"* — brother of the groom, in the register's own words, not\ninferred from the shared surname and shared trade this record had previously (rightly)\nrefused to treat as two identifiers. He is now entered as [[swaelens_franciscus]], son of\n[[swaelens_egidius]] and [[ackermans_joanna]]. **The methodological point stands\nregardless of the outcome**: the earlier refusal was the correct call on the evidence\nthen in hand — a trade running in a family is one correlated signal, not two independent\nones — and it was overturned not by a better score but by reading the document itself.\nThat is the loop working as designed, not a correction of an error.\n\n**A RETRACTION (2026-07-27), against research-log §72.** This record previously stated\nthat \"AGATHA holds a scan for this act (`HUBRA_00197982_0`) but is logged out — confirmed\n`blocked`\". That is wrong and is retracted here. The act's AGATHA page was reopened in a\nlive, logged-in session: the login modal is absent (the session is up), but the page's own\n`modal_URL` field is **empty and hidden**, and there is **no img, Mirador or IIIF\nreference anywhere in the DOM** — the same structural limit already documented for the\nGEWVL West-Vlaanderen birth project (research-log §64/§74). The local harvest confirms it\nindependently: `abl.jsonl`'s record for this act carries **no `SourceAvailableScans` field\nat all**, unlike the 1887 Bossin×Guns act, which does — only a `SourceDigitalOriginal`\npointing at the now end-of-life `search.arch.be`. AGATHA does not publish a scan for this\nact to anyone, logged in or not. This is a `miss` with a stated venue scope, not a\n`blocked` session error, and the search log is corrected to match. The image that settled\nthis record came from the FamilySearch Kraainem film instead (DGS 004716364, frame\n`_00532`).\n\nFRONTIER: any children of this marriage."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"taedts_maria","name":"Maria Taedts","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Taedts","family":"taedts","sex":"f","line":"bundervoet","spouses":[{"id":"bogaert_petrus","name":"Petrus Bogaert"}],"source":"Evergem burial register 1792 — Petronella Bogaert, 31 August 1792","note":"Mother of [[bogaert_petronilla]], and through her a direct ancestor of Renée and Léon.\n\nLike her husband [[bogaert_petrus]] she is here on the strength of one line of the Evergem\nburial register of 31 August 1792, which names her daughter's parents. Nothing else about\nher is stated and nothing else is entered.\n\nTaedts is a rare surname in this corpus, which makes her the better of the two to search\non: a marriage or a burial at Evergem would place the family, where Bogaert alone would\nreturn too many."});
