FamilyTree.person({"id":"vandenberghe_judoca_1594","name":"Judoca Vandenberghe","dates":"~1594 – 2 Sep 1664 Brugge (Lissewege)","confidence":"sup","surname":"Vandenberghe","family":"vandenberghe","born":"~1594","order":"1594","died":"2 Sep 1664 Brugge (Lissewege)","line":"bostyn-cappaert","father":"vandenberghe_carolus","spouses":[{"id":"sabbe_petrus","name":"Petrus Sabbe","detail":"m. Oostkamp (Ruddervoorde), 11 Jun 1616"}],"source":"isavdw — Rijksarchief-sourced, with scan links","note":"Mother of Simon Sabbe (1627). From Ruddervoorde. Her father Carolus Vandenberghe; her mother's identity is uncertain in the records."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"vandenberghe_nn","name":"NN Vandenberghe","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Vandenberghe","family":"vandenberghe","sex":"f","line":"bostyn-cappaert","spouses":[{"id":"sabbe_judocus_1475","name":"Judocus (Joos) Sabbe"}],"source":"isavdw — Rijksarchief-sourced, with scan links","note":"Wife of [[sabbe_judocus_1475]] (~1475–1534) and, on that reading, the mother of\n[[sabbe_petrus_1504]] — though isavdw states the marriage and not the maternity, so\nthe parent link is deliberately not made here.\n\nHer given name is not recorded; only the family name survives. Written the same way\nas [[elisabeth]], the opposite case — a given name and no surname. A name half\nknown is recorded half, never completed.\n\nNo relation to [[vandenberghe_judoca_1594]] is established: that is a different\nVandenberghe, a century later and in a different parish. Same surname is not a link."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"vandenberghe_paulina_1849","name":"Paulina Van Den Berghe","dates":"b. 1849 Hamme (Oost-Vlaanderen)","confidence":"doc","surname":"Van Den Berghe","family":"vandenberghe","born":"1849 Hamme (Oost-Vlaanderen)","order":"1849","line":"de-keyser","father":"vanbergen_joannes_1815","mother":"vermandel_ida_1825","spouses":[{"id":"vermorgen_petrus","name":"Petrus Vermorgen","detail":"m. Hamme (Oost-Vlaanderen), 18 May 1882"}],"source":"Marriage act, 18 May 1882, Hamme (Oost-Vlaanderen) — Petrus Vermorgen × Paulina Van Den Berghe (register image)","note":"An elder sister of [[vanbergen_marielouise_1842]], and the first of that generation this tree has\nbesides her. Her marriage act — **Hamme, 18 May 1882**, read as the register image — names\nher as *meerderjarige dochter van* **Joannes Franciscus Van Den Berghe**, *metser*, sixty-five\nyears old, living at Hamme, present at the wedding and consenting, *en van wijlen* **Ida\nVermandel, overleden te Hamme den eenen twintigste Juny achttien honderd vyf en zestig**.\n\n**What carries the graft is that death date.** Twenty-one June 1865 at Hamme is exactly what\n[[vermandel_ida_1825]]'s own death act says — a different act, a different clerk, seventeen years\napart, and neither knowing about the other. The second identifier is the father's trade,\n*metser*, which is the same trade [[vanbergen_joannes_1815]] is given in [[vanbergen_marielouise_1842]]'s 1888\nOostende death act. Her forename and her mother's surname are not doing the work here.\n\n**It also settles a spelling that was blocking three grafts.** The register writes this\nfamily **Van Bergen** in 1865 — Ida's death act, and the 1865 alphabetical *tafel*, which\nfiles it among Van-acker, Van-Bergen, Van-Bochstael, Van-Bogaert, so it is not a *Van den*\nname there. The 1882 index writes **Van Den Berghe**. That difference was the reason three\npossible siblings sat unrecorded, because a clerk's drift and two families sharing an\nunusually rare wife's surname look identical from an index. Reading the act resolved it as\none family. The surname here follows the act that was actually read.\n\nThe act records her as a widow before this marriage. Her first husband's forename is not\nconfidently legible on the image and no name is recorded from it — a name read at the edge\nof legibility is a guess, and this project has no field for one.\n\n`doc`: the register image was read, by stitching the deep-zoom tiles on the\n`sg30p0.familysearch.org` origin. Her birth year comes from her stated age and is not itself\nproved by an act.\n\nFRONTIER — her own Hamme birth act of about 1849, which would give her birth to the day;\nand her first marriage, which the Hamme registers should hold before May 1882."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"vandenbroeck_elisabeth","name":"Elisabeth van den Broeck","dates":"d. after 1838","confidence":"sup","surname":"van den Broeck","family":"vandenbroeck","died":"after 1838","occupation":"landbouwster (farmer)","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"haesaerts_joannes","name":"Joannes Baptista Haesaerts"}],"source":"Bertem marriage register 1838, act nr. 8, 30 October 1838 — Coekelberghs × Haesaerts (REGISTER IMAGE); Bertem marriage act nr. 8, 30 October 1838 — Coeckelberghs × Haesaerts","note":"Mother of the bride in her daughter [[haesaerts_anna_1810]]'s marriage act — Bertem nr. 8,\n30 October 1838, read as the register image. A *landbouwster* of Berthem, present and\nconsenting in person, and recorded as unable to sign.\n\nThe image writes her surname as **three words**, *van den Broeck*, where AGATHA's\ntranscription runs them together. That is what this record follows, and it is worth\nkeeping: the spacing is what a name index will and will not match on.\n\nNo age in the act, so no birth date here; not called *wijlen*, so the death entry says only\nthat she was alive on the day.\n\n`sup`, not `doc`: see [[coekelberghs_henricus]].\n\nThis is a different woman from [[vandenbroeck_maria]], who is in this tree on another\nline entirely — same surname, no established relation, and the two records are deliberately\nnot linked."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"vandenbroeck_maria","name":"Maria Theresia Van den Broeck","dates":"d. before 1946","confidence":"doc","surname":"Van den Broeck","family":"vandenbroeck","died":"before 1946","line":"de-keyser","spouses":[{"id":"dekeyser_desiderius","name":"Desiderius De Keyser"}],"source":"Édouard Dekeyser's 1946 Oostende remarriage act (akte nr. 81); FamilySearch","note":"Source detail: Oostende marriage act nr. 81, 9 May 1946 (FamilySearch, Belgium West Flanders Civil Registration 1582-1950 — image read directly), naming her as mother of Eduardus De Keyser\n\nMOTHER of Édouard Dekeyser. Named in Édouard's 1946 remarriage act as his mother, 'overleden' (deceased) by then. Van den Broeck is a Waasland / Hamme (Oost-Vlaanderen) name, consistent with the family's Hamme origin. Frontier: her dates/parents, in the Hamme (O.-Vl.) registers."});
